  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 415 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nAs of 3PM, a cold front is currently bisecting CWA in roughly\nhalf from SW to NE. Behind the front, winds have turned to the\nnorth and temperatures are generally in the upper 60s to 70s.\nSouth of the front, it remains unseasonably hot in the 80s to\nlower 90s. Smith Center is even as high as 97F! Despite the\nproximity to the front, there's hardly a single cloud in the\nentire CWA.\n\nMain item of concern in the short term is the potential for\nscattered showers and storms to develop across portions of north\ncentral Kansas this evening and overnight as a strengthening low\nlevel jet begins to interact with the elevated front. HRRR\ntrends have been fairly consistent in this idea, however, HREF \nprobabilities are only 20-30%. Probably looking at mainly a 6-hr\nwindow from 02Z to 08Z before the LLJ veers to the E and the\nfront continues to progress deeper into KS.\n\nFor Tuesday, the vast majority of the CWA's sensible weather\nwill be cloudy and cool with a chilly (esp. compared to today!)\nnorth breeze...but mainly dry. The exception could be across the\nfar S/SE where convection may once again develop atop the\nelevated portion of the front. The surface cold front should lie\nwell SE of the area by peak heating - generally along the I-35\ncorridor - such that the primary severe threat remains off to\nour SE. Can't completely rule out a few stronger cores taping\ninto lingering elevated instability and strong effective deep \nlayer shear, so SPC has continued to highlight far SE zones in a\n\"Marginal Risk\" (level 1 of 5) in their latest outlook.\n\nTo go along with the trend for a more southern placement of the\nsurface cold front, have lowered highs across the board as \npersistent and stubborn low clouds and aforementioned cool Nrly\nbreeze will make it quite difficult to warm up. Also likely \nsome systematic bias-correction issues with the NBM (for high \ntemperatures), as there often is during the transition months. \nFar N zones may struggle to warm out of the 50s, with mid 60s to\nmid 70s expected elsewhere (from N to S).\n\nThe main time frame for precipitation for the vast majority of\nthe area will be Wednesday into Thursday. Expect a broad area of\nprecip to gradually expand from SW to NE during the day on\nWednesday, peak in coverage and intensity Wednesday night into\nThursday AM, then gradually taper off from W to E Thursday\nafternoon and evening. Latest EPS 25-75th percentile QPF\ngenerally lies in the 0.25-0.75\" range, though some higher\nmembers (including the deterministic run) have pockets of \nhigher values in the 1-1.5\" range. May have some convective \nelements/lightning work into portions of northern KS Wed-Wed\nnight, but should be a mainly non-convective, steady rain for \nNE locations. Thursday AM will be quite wet, but should see a\nfairly quick downturn in coverage and intensity as we go through\nthe afternoon and early evening hours. Blustery N wind Thu night\nshould keep us from really bottoming out on temperatures and\navoid frost for at least one more night.\n\nForecast dries back out for the end of the work week and into \nthe weekend. Seasonably cool conditions continue into Friday \nand Saturday...current forecast highs for Friday are only in the\nmid 50s to low 60s. Our first frost (perhaps even a localized\nfreeze?) of the season is becoming increasingly likely for \nFriday night into Saturday morning. Current NBM forecast has \nlight winds, mostly clear skies, and temperatures dropping into \nthe 30s area-wide. Some guidance is even colder in the 20s, \nwhich does not seem unreasonable for more cold-prone locations \nsuch as Dawson County northeastward towards Ord and Greeley.",
